  "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3/2}-{0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(1-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
